The new English abstract* section should be:

\begin{abstract*}
  Quantum geometry characterizes the intrinsic structure of Bloch wave functions in momentum space. Its central object---the quantum geometric tensor---decomposes into the Berry curvature (imaginary part) and the quantum metric (real part), which respectively govern topological invariants and interband coherence effects, and together shape the classification and transport properties of topological quantum matter. This dissertation investigates the role of quantum geometry in condensed matter systems along three interconnected directions: static topological diagnosis, weak-field nonlinear response, and strong-field nonequilibrium dynamics, employing symmetry analysis, effective model construction, and first-principles calculations.

  First, we generalize the nested Wilson loop formalism to crystalline systems with arbitrary space group symmetries, overcoming the long-standing limitation to symmorphic (e.g., mirror) symmetries. By introducing generalized Wannier sector partitioning and generalized nested Berry phases as bulk indicators of higher-order topology, we systematically analyze the symmetry constraints on Wannier bands across all 80 layer groups, identifying 9 groups supporting conventional and 15 supporting generalized nested Berry phases. The latter cover non-symmorphic orthorhombic, tetragonal, and hexagonal lattice types. Through first-principles calculations on the Dirac semimetals NaCuSe and KMgBi, we establish the correspondence between generalized nested Berry phases and higher-order Fermi arcs, demonstrating that jumps of the generalized nested Berry phase near Dirac points determine the existence and termination of higher-order Fermi arcs. Notably, in KMgBi the higher-order topological phase transition is captured by the generalized nested Berry phase but missed by the conventional one.

  Second, we study the intrinsic nonlinear Hall effect (INH) driven by the quantum metric in two-dimensional antiferromagnets with $\mathcal{PT}$ symmetry, where the Berry curvature dipole mechanism is symmetry-forbidden. Taking monolayer MnS as a representative platform, first-principles calculations reveal INH conductivities on the order of nm$\cdot$mA/V$^2$, exhibiting a $2\pi$-periodic dependence on the N\'eel vector orientation that enables all-electrical readout of the antiferromagnetic order parameter. In a complementary study on ZrTe$_5$ thin films, we show that interlayer sliding triggers spontaneous inversion symmetry breaking, simultaneously inducing slide ferroelectricity and pronounced Berry curvature and orbital moment distributions. The resulting nonlinear anomalous Hall effect and kinetic magnetoelectric effect couple directly to the ferroelectric polarization, serving as sensitive probes of surface sliding.

  Third, we propose a novel oscillation mechanism induced by the interband quantum metric under strong electric fields. By establishing a recursive density-matrix framework in the large-gap approximation, we unify Bloch oscillations, Berry curvature oscillations, and quantum metric oscillations within a single theoretical scheme. The interband coherence contribution from the second-order density matrix yields a quantum-metric-driven steady drift current that persists even in strictly flat bands and scales linearly with the electric field under time-reversal or inversion symmetry---in sharp contrast to the inverse-field decay of Bloch drift. In the time-dependent regime, the breathing-mode signal of quantum metric oscillations does not diminish with increasing field strength, providing a direct experimental criterion for distinguishing different oscillation mechanisms. Numerical simulations on one-dimensional superlattice and two-dimensional honeycomb lattice models confirm these predictions, suggesting epitaxial superlattices, moir\'e superlattices, optical lattices, and cold-atom systems as promising experimental platforms.

